To implement the Sobel edge detection algorithm with Gaussian filtering in MATLAB, you need to first apply the Gaussian filter to the input image and then apply the Sobel operator on the filtered image. Here's the modified code for your reference:

```MATLAB
% Read input image
input_image = imread('image.jpg');

% Convert to grayscale
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);

% Apply Gaussian filter
kernel_size = [3 3];
sigma = 1;
gaussian_filtered_image = fspecial('gaussian', kernel_size, sigma) * gray_image;

% Define Sobel operator in x direction
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];

% Define Sobel operator in y direction
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];

% Apply Sobel operator in x direction
edge_image_x = conv2(double(gaussian_filtered_image), sobel_x, 'same');

% Apply Sobel operator in y direction
edge_image_y = conv2(double(gaussian_filtered_image), sobel_y, 'same');

% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);

% Display edge image
imshow(edge_image);
```

In this modified code, we first apply a Gaussian filter using the `fspecial` function. The `kernel_size` specifies the size of the kernel (in this case, a 3x3 square kernel), and the `sigma` parameter controls the standard deviation of the Gaussian distribution. After applying the Gaussian filter, we proceed with the Sobel edge detection algorithm as before.

