Here's an example of how you can create an Apps Script that searches for a place name in a column and stores the latitude and longitude in other columns:

```javascript
function searchPlace() {
  var sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSpreadsheet().getActiveSheet();
  var dataRange = sheet.getRange("A2:A"); // Assuming your data starts in cell A2
  var placeName = dataRange.getValue();

  var geocoder = new google.maps.Geocoder();
  geocoder.geocode({ address: placeName }, function(results, status) {
    if (status == google.maps.GeocoderStatus.OK) {
      var latitude = results[0].geometry.location.lat();
      var longitude = results[0].geometry.location.lng();

      sheet.getRange("B2").setValue(latitude); // Store latitude in column B
      sheet.getRange("C2").setValue(longitude); // Store longitude in column C
    } else {
      sheet.getRange("B2").setValue("Invalid place name"); // Set an error message in column B
      sheet.getRange("C2").setValue(""); // Clear the value in column C
    }
  });
}
```

To use this script, follow these steps:

1. Open your Google Sheet.
2. Click on "Extensions" > "Apps Script."
3. Copy and paste the code into the script editor.
4. Save the script by clicking on the floppy disk icon in the top left corner.
5. To run the script, click on the play button (⏸) in the top left corner.
6. The script will search for the place name in the first cell of column A and store the latitude and longitude in cells B2 and C2, respectively. If the place name is invalid, an error message will be displayed in cell B2, and the value in cell C2 will be cleared.

Note: You'll need to enable the Google Maps API in your Google Sheet by going to "Tools" > "Script editor" > "Resources" > "Advanced Google services" and enabling the "Google Maps JavaScript API."

Here's the PHP custom function "smart_sort_array" as requested:

```php
function smart_sort_array($set, $order) {
    usort($set, function ($a, $b) use ($order) {
        if ($a['age'] == $b['age']) {
            return 0;
        }
        return $order === 'asc' ? $a['age'] - $b['age'] : $b['age'] - $a['age'];
    });
    return $set;
}
```

To use this function, simply call it with your set and order parameters like this:

```php
$set = [
    ['name' => 'John', 'address' => 'New York', 'age' => 30],
    ['name' => 'Jane', 'address' => 'Los Angeles', 'age' => 25],
    ['name' => 'Bob', 'address' => 'Chicago', 'age' => 35]
];

$sorted_set = smart_sort_array($set, 'asc'); // Sort in ascending order
$sorted_set = smart_sort_array($set, 'desc'); // Sort in descending order

print_r($sorted_set);
```

```javascript
function getOptionEntries(obj, key) {
  const entries = [];
  Object.entries(obj).forEach(([nestedKey, value]) => {
    if (nestedKey === key) {
      entries.push(value);
    } else if (typeof value === 'object' && value !== null) {
      entries.push(...getOptionEntries(value, key));
    }
  });
  return entries;
}
```

This function takes two arguments: `obj`, which is the object to search, and `key`, which is the string key to match. It uses recursion to traverse the object and find all entries where the nested key value matches the given string key. The function returns an array of all matching values.

